  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 145 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe only change up front for the long term forecast is an earlier\ntiming that is showing up for the storm system expected to move \ninto the CWA by Thursday. Otherwise there has been very little \nchange from the previous forecasts. The weekend still looks to be \non the colder side, especially overnight with potential for a \nfreeze.\n\nStarting Wednesday, we have a relatively warm air mass in place. \nMoisture will be on the increase, but the best surface moisture \nmay still lie further to the south, keeping things dry across the \nCWA. There is some minor uncertainty here worth keeping in mind \ngiven that the cut-off low over Texas will be progressing across \nthe area, but again, moisture appears to be limited which should \nprevent any rain. Highs will be in the 70s area-wide, which \ntranslates to 5-10 degrees above average.\n\nOvernight, cloud cover will be on the increase ahead of the \napproaching system. Aloft, shortwave will dig into the area with a\nbroader trough. Ensembles on beginning to agree that the best \nsurface pressure falls will occur further to the north across the \nOhio Valley. This will limit moisture uptake for the system. \nStill, it will have some access, and it looks like north Georgia \nwill be the best place to see some rain late Wednesday into early \non Thursday morning. As noted above, this is definitely earlier \ntiming than what was shown yesterday, and there is ensemble \nagreement around this (beware of under-dispersion). Overall \nrainfall amounts don't look concerning (generally well under half \nan inch) given the lack of moisture and instability during this \ntime period. A front pushes into the area on Thursday, so \ntemperatures may remain similar to the morning temperature through\nthe day across parts of north Georgia, while central (especially \neast central) Georgia will see highs climb into the 70s again \nahead of the front.\n\nSome wrap around precip continues to be possible with the larger \nlow that develops off the coast and moves into the northeast \nThursday night into Friday. This will need to be monitored as \ntemps are forecast to be near freezing in the higher elevations. \nProbability continues to be low, but not zero, for some frozen \nprecip at some of the highest areas of the Georgia mountains.\n\nInto the weekend, cold air moves in with the surface high \npressure. We swing the other direction with high temperatures in \nthe 50s and low 60s Friday and Saturday. These highs will be 5 to \n15 degrees below average, and some locations in the mountains may \nbe as much as 20 degrees below average. Weather will be dry \notherwise. This will likely bring a freeze to much of north \nGeorgia and the Atlanta metro, with a hard freeze possible in some\nlocations. The growing season is at or near the end, so this \nisn't too unexpected for this time of year.",
